  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 129 PM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nHeavy Rainfall Potential through Saturday: Rain associated with a\nlow pressure system emerging from the Plains is having issues \nmaking steady progress into the area given the abundant amount of \nlow level moisture being pumped in from a high pressure system \nover western Quebec. The initial surge of moisture should moisten \nthe low levels through this afternoon, which will set the stage \nfor steadier rain to make its way through the region later this \nafternoon and into tonight. This rain will last through Saturday \nwhen the low tracks to our south, coming to an end Saturday night.\nThe heaviest rain still looks to be just to our south coincident \nwith the best instability and frontogenesis. While there are still\nsome models that edge the heavier rain into the southern CWA, the\ngeneral trend and consensus is to keep the higher amounts to our \nsouth. The southern solution also looks to be the most reasonable \ngiven we mostly miss out on instability from this event, which is \nneeded to produce the heavier rain showers.\n\nQuiet Weather Next Week: Looking ahead to next week, shortwave\nenergy originating from the western North American trough will\nslowly migrate east from the central Plains to the southern Great\nLakes and eventually the east coast. Therefore, it appears that\nmost of the rain will remain south of the region. Otherwise, \nstrong high pressure over northern Ontario and northern Quebec \nlooks to keep ample dry air across northern Wisconsin which should\nkeep next week mostly dry. \n\nWith high pressure maintaining dry and quiet conditions for the\nbulk of the extended period, temperatures will be undergoing a\nslow warming trend. Daytime highs ranging from the low to mid 60s\non Monday will gradually warm each day, reaching the low to mid\n70s by Thursday and next Friday.",
